1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
Now, since we get to saying, you know, while we're on this topic,

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:06,620
so once you're doing index funds and you're saying, well,

3
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:08,820
we're doing this in order to get this diversification,

4
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:12,740
they're like one of the other big lessons that,

5
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:16,420
practical lessons that's come out of financial economics is

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:18,300
that there's something called home bias.

7
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:24,620
Where human beings in pretty much every country

8
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:27,580
around the world tend to invest a strangely large amount

9
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:28,240
in their home country.

10
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,220
There are a few countries that don't?

11
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:31,800
No, no, no.

12
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:32,340
That's crazy.

13
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:33,480
Yeah. Yes.

14
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:36,000
So even if you're like in Belgium or something,

15
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,000
there's like you might have like 30%

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,240
of your assets in Belgian assets.

17
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:40,060
It's not that much.

18
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:43,060
Like, you know how small a part of the world Belgium is?

19
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:44,980
You have 30% of your assets in Belgium?

20
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:47,180
How many people who aren't Belgian have 30%

21
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:48,640
of their assets in Belgian assets?

22
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,100
Like, almost no one would do it.

23
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,720
So, this is a very real problem.

24
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,240
And even in the United States, most estimates say

25
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:00,740
that you should never have even 50% or more of your assets

26
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:04,380
in US, of your wealth in US assets.

27
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:08,100
So, when we talk about diversifying away risk,

28
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,320
there's a lot of risk that people could be diversifying

29
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:12,060
away that they don't bother diversifying away.

30
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:16,620
Right? And, again, of course, back in earlier times

31
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:18,960
when it was just hard to go and invest internationally,

32
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:20,140
there might have been some excuse.

33
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:22,300
But now it's so easy to do this.

34
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,340
And there are index funds for many different countries,

35
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:27,860
for many different regions of the world.

36
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:29,680
Right? And then when you think a little bit more

37
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:31,900
about how most people own a home in the country

38
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:34,120
where they reside, right?

39
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:37,740
Normally the home that you own is in the country where you live.

40
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:40,160
You, like, the percentage of your assets

41
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:42,660
that you actually have in your home country is probably,

42
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:44,080
is way higher than you think.

43
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:47,140
Because, you know, even if you put half

44
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:51,140
of your liquid financial assets outside of your country,

45
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:53,640
if your home is half your wealth, then you still have,

46
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:56,080
like, three quarters of your wealth in your home country.

47
00:01:56,580 --> 00:01:57,840
Which, again, is pretty crazy.

48
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:03,400
Right? And, again, it's so easy for people to go and try

49
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:04,780
to move their assets in other places.

50
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:07,400
So this is something where, again,

51
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,560
I will not claim any independent wisdom on this.

52
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:11,780
But when I read it, like, my God, this is totally right.

53
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:14,740
And, see, my wife was a little nervous about it.

54
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:15,960
But then I said, well, here's what we'll do.

55
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:19,800
We'll make a phantom portfolio that I'm advocating,

56
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,000
which we'll just put down and pretend we own it.

57
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:22,720
We don't really own it.

58
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:24,400
But we'll just put it down so you can see what happens.

59
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:26,800
And then after a year, okay, this doesn't seem too crazy.

60
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:32,100
So now I've got maybe down to, like, 25% U.S. assets.

61
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:32,820
Maybe even less.

62
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:34,620
So that's only financial markets.

63
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:35,820
So we still own a house here.

64
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:37,320
So maybe I should go even lower.

65
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:41,420
Right? Now, you know, and then when you're thinking

66
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:46,120
about this more, like, when you owe, like,

67
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:49,040
any time that you owe an asset that are in another country,

68
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,300
that any time your own country's currency crashes,

69
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:54,360
that's actually awesome for you.

70
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,760
Right? So suppose that the dollar were to fall to 1%

71
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:00,660
of its current value compared to the pound.

72
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:04,720
This would mean that the value of my British assets in terms

73
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,840
of what I can buy here in the U.S. would multiply.

74
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:07,840
What did I say?

75
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:10,240
Fell 99% would be worth 100 times as much.

76
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:11,860
So that would be pretty cool.

77
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:14,660
So, and normally I'm rooting for the dollar to crash

78
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:18,860
because I own all these foreign assets,

79
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:22,340
but I consume almost everything in the U.S. So,

80
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:24,440
at least we're thinking about it.

81
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:25,760
All right.

82
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:28,300
So questions, questions.

83
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:29,680
All right.

84
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:32,080
So, all right.

85
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:33,840
Moving on to calendar effects.

86
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:38,600
So even though the efficient markets hypothesis surpasses

87
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,940
some pretty nice empirical tests, and it's just impressive,

88
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:45,620
just realizing like you cannot predict the annual movements

89
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:48,140
in stock indices just using past movements,

90
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:50,420
which I think is a very popular view.

91
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:52,700
If there is a view, well, like good years go together,

92
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:53,760
bad years go together.

93
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:55,540
Yeah, they do, except when they don't.

94
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:58,420
Except when you have a good year,

95
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:01,420
then you have a terrible year, which wipes out the correlation.

96
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:03,460
And then it's like, well, but as long

97
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:05,520
as you know what the turning point is, yeah,

98
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:06,520
well if you could know that.

99
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:08,620
I think once the economists actually did a thought

100
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:12,500
experiment and just said, imagine a person every year just

101
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:14,500
knew which country would have the largest increase

102
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:15,540
in stock value that year.

103
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:19,000
How rich would a person be if every year they could just move

104
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,980
their assets to the country that would go up the most?

105
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:24,720
Yes. And the answer is that person would own more

106
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:26,640
than the world if they could have done this.

107
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:29,360
And that's not nearly as hard

108
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:31,400
as picking the best stock every year or anything.

109
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:32,920
This is just picking the right country.

110
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:37,680
Even that, if you could really do it, you would be richer

111
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:40,440
than any human being is by far.

112
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:43,160
So something's like, how hard can it be

113
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:44,640
to know which country's going to be best on earth?

114
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:45,820
Super hard.

115
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:48,160
So hard, no human being has done it ever.

116
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:49,340
It's that hard.

117
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:51,420
No human being's come remotely close.

118
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:54,800
I guess if someone every now and then, they just pick a country

119
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:57,000
where they lose all their money, then that would work,

120
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,820
because then you'd have a zero multiplied in there,

121
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:01,460
and then it would fit with the fact that you own nothing.

122
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:04,240
But yes. All right.

123
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:07,960
So anyway, there's a bunch of, but there are a bunch

124
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:10,580
of anomalies for the MH, and some of the ones

125
00:05:10,580 --> 00:05:12,060
that have gotten the most attention are called

126
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:12,880
calendar effects.

127
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:16,040
So calendar effects.

128
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:18,220
Excuse me.

129
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:24,160
Effects. And the best note

130
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:27,200
of all these is the legendary January effect.

131
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:28,980
The January effect.

132
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:35,300
So, Thaler Reports, book published, I think,

133
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:37,040
like around 99 or so.

134
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:39,660
I don't remember when it was, when it was first published.

135
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:41,720
So thereabouts, I think.

136
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:44,400
So back then, he predicted, he said that, you know,

137
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:46,600
average New York Stock Exchange monthly returns from February

138
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:48,560
to December are.5% per month.

139
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:51,220
Average January return is 3.5%.

140
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:55,120
So there's one month that has seven times the normal rate

141
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:55,520
of return.

142
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:57,440
All right.

143
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:01,480
And if you look at it further, it seems like it primarily comes

144
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:05,420
from these really high returns for small firms in January.

145
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:09,940
So you just buy, invest in the small firms in January,

146
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:13,960
and you get over half your annual return right then.

147
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:14,540
I think, let's see.

148
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:15,100
Or no, no, no.

149
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:15,440
Let's see.

150
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:16,140
Would it be over half?

151
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:17,580
No, it wouldn't be quite that good.

152
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:17,900
Let's see.

153
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:20,720
Eleven. So, you know, you got like two-thirds

154
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:21,400
of your annual return.

155
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:23,440
Or like one-third of your annual return, but still pretty good.

156
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:24,820
All right.

157
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:26,980
And then you might think this is just a U.S. specific thing.

158
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:29,440
So, of course, this inspired a lot of research.

159
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:32,060
And 15 out of 16 countries studied had a January effect

160
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:33,240
by the late 90s.

161
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:36,740
All right.

162
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:40,880
Now, since then, many people have, you know,

163
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:42,880
people working in this area have generally said the January

164
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:45,420
effect has either gone away or is going away.

165
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:47,120
So I haven't kept up to date

166
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:48,900
on what the very latest word on it is.

167
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:52,400
But a common saying is the January effect is now happening

168
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:53,560
in December.

169
00:06:54,260 --> 00:06:55,200
Right? And that's the idea.

170
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,560
Well, if everybody knows that it's going to go up in January,

171
00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:58,540
then it won't go up in January.

172
00:06:58,540 --> 00:06:59,340
It'll go up in December.

173
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:02,860
But I think actually there's just been a general annual,

174
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:05,540
general smoothing over calendar months.

175
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:07,640
But I haven't followed that closely.

176
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:09,880
So I'm not sure what the current story is.

177
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:11,760
But even if it goes away totally,

178
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,260
the fact that it was big for many decades and 15

179
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:19,260
out of 16 countries studied is not the kind of thing you can

180
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:20,440
just say, ah, it doesn't mean anything.

181
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,660
It's a split.

182
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:23,740
So that's one case.

183
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:27,860
Another one that is not nearly as impressive

184
00:07:27,860 --> 00:07:30,320
but still well-documented is something called the

185
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:30,960
weekend effect.

186
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:33,740
So when you go and leave your money in a bank account

187
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:36,360
with continuous compounding over the weekend,

188
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:39,880
how much interest do you get between Friday and Monday?

189
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:44,300
So continuous compounding.

190
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,940
So between Friday morning and Monday morning,

191
00:07:48,940 --> 00:07:50,540
how many days have passed?

192
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:52,300
Three days.

193
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:53,840
So with continuous compounding,

194
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:54,820
how many days interest do you get?

195
00:07:55,460 --> 00:07:56,560
Three. All right.

196
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:57,480
So pretty obvious.

197
00:07:57,980 --> 00:07:58,260
All right.

198
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:01,300
Now it would seem like stock markets would also have

199
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:02,500
continuous compounding, right?

200
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:06,460
So people are always able to go and buy and sell.

201
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:14,640
So anyway, people could go and dump their portfolio on Friday,

202
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:16,420
buy something with continuous compounding,

203
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:19,380
and then sell the continuously compounded thing on Monday

204
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:22,040
morning and then buy back their portfolio

205
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:24,420
if they weren't getting the continuously compounded return

206
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:25,180
on the stocks.

207
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:27,880
So it seems like the average Friday

208
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:29,660
and Monday return should be three times the normal return.

209
00:08:30,820 --> 00:08:32,420
Right? But actually,

210
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:34,240
Monday returns don't seem to be especially good.

211
00:08:35,380 --> 00:08:36,900
So that's another thing that's been found.

212
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:42,120
Now, Thaler, who again is, if you go and read him carefully,

213
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:43,980
he's always more reasonable than the summary.

214
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:45,440
All right.

215
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:46,640
So, all right.

216
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:49,660
And possibly more reasonable than his own summary when he's

217
00:08:49,660 --> 00:08:50,940
on TV or something like that.

218
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:54,260
So, you know, I have been on TV, so yes,

219
00:08:54,260 --> 00:08:56,020
you do have to simplify, but still.

220
00:08:56,420 --> 00:08:59,620
If you do read him, he says something that is much richer

221
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:01,200
than the view that is normally attributed to him.

222
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:03,940
So one thing he admits is, look, sure,

223
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:05,760
these anomalies are pretty hard to take advantage

224
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:07,020
of due to transactions costs.

225
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:11,380
But what he says is, you know, at least you should expect people

226
00:09:11,380 --> 00:09:13,240
to adjust the timing of the transactions.

227
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:15,360
In order to take advantage of the pattern.

228
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:17,560
So if you know that you're going to buy stocks once a year,

229
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:19,700
there's a January effect, when should you buy the stocks?

230
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:21,900
By the end of December.

231
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:23,640
Right? And then either way,

232
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:24,880
you're paying the transactions costs.

233
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:26,140
But then in one case, you're going

234
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:27,100
and getting your best deal.

235
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:28,600
Whereas if you just do it randomly,

236
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:30,220
maybe the dice will come out February.

237
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:32,000
And then you're getting a way lower return.

238
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:33,880
And you pay the same transactions costs.

239
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:36,860
All right.

240
00:09:36,860 --> 00:09:39,580
And yes, so similarly, so there would be a problem

241
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:42,700
in the real world in selling all your stocks on Friday morning

242
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:44,660
and then buying them back on Monday morning.

243
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:46,660
Or Friday evening and then buying them back Monday morning.

244
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:48,960
Which is almost every place would go and charge a fee,

245
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:50,080
which would wipe out the benefits.

246
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:52,260
You see, like, if you really know what you're doing,

247
00:09:52,260 --> 00:09:55,440
you might be able to have a, one of the brokerages

248
00:09:55,440 --> 00:09:58,740
where they just charge a flat amount per sale order

249
00:09:58,740 --> 00:09:59,820
or purchase order.

250
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:03,200
And then if you, and then if you don't diversify as much,

251
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:04,400
you might go, you might be able

252
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:06,780
to get the transactions costs down.

253
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:08,120
If you have an index fund,

254
00:10:08,120 --> 00:10:10,940
normally they actually will not allow you to do stuff like that.

255
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:13,560
So normally for index funds, there's a rule saying

256
00:10:13,560 --> 00:10:15,400
that you're not allowed to buy or sell more often

257
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:16,580
than every few months.

258
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:19,440
And actually, when you call Redeem,

259
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:23,140
the last few times I called, they said, why are you selling?

260
00:10:23,860 --> 00:10:26,060
And if you say, to market time, then they'll say, oh,

261
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:28,180
I'm sorry, sir, you're not allowed to do that.

262
